<p>Austin is a city of trees: live oaks that sprawl across whole lots, cedar elms, pecans, bald cypress along the creeks, and the smaller ornamentals that fill newer subdivisions. The best <strong>landscape lighting ideas for trees</strong> respect how each species is built, because a technique that makes a live oak look monumental can make a crape myrtle look like a lollipop. Below is how the designers at Austin Landscape Lighting approach trees, from the biggest canopy to the smallest accent.</p>

<h2>Why trees come first in a lighting design</h2>
<p>A lit tree does three jobs at once. It gives the property height and depth that a lit facade alone cannot. It reflects soft light back onto the lawn and patio so you need fewer fixtures elsewhere. And it is the view from inside the house, which is where you spend most of the evening. We often start a design at the tree you see from the kitchen sink.</p>

<h2>Uplighting: the classic</h2>
<p>Fixtures at the base of the trunk aim up into the canopy. The rules that separate professional results from a bright blob:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Use two or three fixtures on any tree over 15 feet</strong>, placed at different angles so one side never goes flat. A single uplight creates a hard shadow line and a bald back side.</li>
  <li><strong>Match the beam to the canopy.</strong> Narrow 15 to 24 degree beams punch up tall, upright trees like cypress and cedar elm. Wider 36 to 60 degree beams fill the broad crown of a live oak.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep fixtures 2 to 4 feet off the trunk</strong> for a textured bark wash, further for a softer canopy glow. Too close and only the bark lights; too far and the beam spills past the tree.</li>
  <li><strong>Output: 4 to 7 watts LED</strong> for most residential trees, up to 9 or 12 watts for a 50-foot pecan. More than that reads as a parking lot.</li>
</ul>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/landscape-lighting-ideas-trees-1.webp" alt="Three uplights cross-lighting a large oak in Austin" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="900"><figcaption>Cross-lighting from three angles keeps the canopy round and the trunk textured.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Moonlighting: light from inside the canopy</h2>
<p>This is the technique Austin homeowners ask about by name after they see it at a neighbor's. A fixture is mounted high in the tree, 20 to 35 feet up, aimed down through the branches. Leaves and limbs break the beam into dappled shadows that move with the wind, exactly like moonlight. Use a wide 60-degree beam, a slightly cooler 3000K lamp if you want a true moon tone or 2700K to match the rest of the yard, and a long shroud so the source stays hidden from below. Mount with stand-offs and leave a service loop so the tree can grow. One moonlight can light a patio, a path and a lawn at once, which makes it the most efficient idea on this page. It is a core part of our <a href="/services/garden-and-tree-lighting/">garden and tree lighting</a> service.</p>

<h2>Silhouetting and shadowing</h2>
<p>For trees with strong outlines, such as a Japanese maple, a yucca or a twisted mountain laurel, put the light behind the plant and wash the wall or fence beyond it. The tree shows as a dark shape against a lit surface. Shadowing is the reverse: light the plant from the front so it throws a magnified shadow onto the wall. Both work best on smaller trees within 6 feet of a flat surface and both are ways to light a tree with only one fixture.</p>

