<h2>Grouping trees and the lawn between them</h2>
<p>With several trees, decide which is the star and give it the most light. Secondary trees get one soft fixture each or none, so the eye moves across the yard in a hierarchy instead of a line of equal spotlights. Leave the lawn between them dark on purpose; the contrast is what makes the lit trees look dramatic.</p>

<h2>Mistakes we fix most often</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>One fixture per tree, straight up.</strong> Flat, with a hot spot on the trunk.</li>
  <li><strong>Fixtures too bright.</strong> A 35-watt halogen on a 12-foot crape myrtle. Swap to a 3-watt LED and the bark appears.</li>
  <li><strong>Blue light.</strong> 4000K and up turns oak leaves gray. Stay at 2700K to 3000K.</li>
  <li><strong>Glare into windows.</strong> Walk inside and check every fixture. A shroud or a few degrees of re-aim fixes most.</li>
  <li><strong>No plan for growth.</strong> Trees get wider; fixtures need to move out over the years. Leave slack in the wire and expect a re-aim visit every couple of seasons, which our <a href="/services/maintenance-and-repair/">maintenance plans</a> cover.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Power, wire and transformer for tree lighting</h2>
<p>Trees are usually the furthest fixtures from the house, so voltage drop matters. Run 12-gauge wire to a hub at the tree and short leads to each fixture, or use 10-gauge for runs over 150 feet. A pair of oaks with three uplights and a moonlight each draws about 50 watts; size the transformer with 25 percent headroom using the <a href="/tools/transformer-calculator/">transformer tool</a>. Moonlight wiring should run up the shaded back of the trunk with UV-stable cable and stainless or brass hardware.</p>

<h2>See it on your trees</h2>
<p>Trees are the one feature that cannot be judged from a catalog photo; the same fixture looks different on every canopy. Austin Landscape Lighting brings demo fixtures to a free dusk consultation and lights your actual trees before you decide on anything. <a href="/contact/">Book a consultation</a> and see what your oaks have been hiding.</p>
